    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i checked the interface stats on 192.168.40.1 and there aren't any obvious CRC errors or drops on the physical port itself. so it doesn't look like a bad cable or a failing SFP. \n\nbut the jitter is still crazy. like it'll be fine for 10 minutes and then just spike out of nowhere. could it be something with the MTU settings or maybe some weird interference on the line? what else should i look for?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the physical layer looks clean, the problem is likely higher up the stack, which makes the intermittent nature of the spikes very typical of traffic management issues. You should absolutely check the MTU settings across the path, as fragmentation issues can cause unpredictable delays, especially when packets are being retransmitted. Furthermore, look closely at the QoS (Quality of Service) settings on the router at 192.168.40.1; if traffic is being aggressively policed or shaped, it can cause those sudden, jarring spikes in jitter.",
      "source": "target",
